{n}The collar lay on the floor where she had let it fall. Horzalah drew the Commander's hand back up the length of her throat and pressed it flat over the ridged scar, and held it there with her whole weight behind her fingers.{/n}
"Still. You reached for this like a buyer, once, to see what you were buying. Feel it now. This is what it is to be given something." {n}Her pulse beat quick under the Commander's palm. She watched the Commander's face while it did, and smiled to see the Commander learn.{/n}
{n}She had the Commander's shirt off by its collar and the Commander on the bed by the shoulder, and then she did nothing at all. She stood over the Commander in the lamplight, lean and hot and unhurried, and let the Commander look, and let the Commander ache to touch, and did not allow it. Her fingers drew the Commander's wrist up above the pillow and pinned it there. "I decide where your hands go, mortal. I decide when."{/n}
{n}It was a long time before she allowed it. Then she set the Commander's palm on her hip and her teeth in the Commander's lip together, and the Commander's answer, when it came, got a hiss and no complaint. Each time the Commander moved without leave she stopped, and waited, and made the Commander wait with her, breathing hard, until the Commander had learned what she wanted. When the Commander had, her own head went back, and the scar at her throat stood out pale in the lamplight.{/n}
"Mine," {n}she said against the ruined edge where the ear had been, her tongue tracing the scar as if checking her own signature.{/n} "The ear. The mouth. The hands I put where I please. All mine." {n}Her nails opened the skin of the Commander's shoulder. When the peak took her she swore in the tongue of the Abyss, and only then, unhurried, as she took everything, did she let the Commander follow.{/n}
{n}She lay across the Commander afterwards with her cheek against the Commander's chest, listening, and would have denied it if asked. She said it had been the slowest lesson she had ever given, and that the Commander was not to expect another until she chose to teach it.{/n}
{n}By morning the collar was buckled again, and she went back to her Guild the way she had come, through no door at all.{/n}
